WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.900
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.710
I am Priyanka, and
in this episode,

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.010
we'll share the recipe
to set up a cold DR

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.320
pattern for applications
that are primarily

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.060
deployed on premise.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.470
So let's dive in.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.362
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:29.060
Your DR plan would depend
on your specific application

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.440
and recovery goals.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.500
Let's consider a few
scenarios to understand this.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.080
If you run batch
processing workloads,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.940
they tend to be
non-mission critical

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.850
and don't need to be designed
for high availability.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.970
In such cases, you would use
pre-emptable virtual machine

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.660
instances in conjunction
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.760
And that's your DR plan.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.150
If you run an e-commerce site,
then the actual purchasing

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.280
pipeline needs to have
high availability,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.540
but the email process that
sends those notifications

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.510
to customers can
tolerate a bit of delay.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:07.280
This is a mix of hot purchasing
and warm or cold notification

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.300
patterns.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.120
If you have a video
streaming solution, then

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.810
from the search to
actually streaming content,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.810
the entire experience
needs to be HA

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.370
or the customer might
turn to your competitor.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:24.330
Well, let's talk about an
interesting company, Main

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.840
Street Art.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.120
They run their
application on premise

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.660
and are building a DR
infrastructure on Google Cloud.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.270
They are now working
out a disaster recovery

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.520
plan with the low budget and are
OK with a bit high RPO and RTO

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.760
values.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.320
This means they need to
set up a cooled DR pattern.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.320
In cooled DR pattern,
Main Street Art

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.370
needs minimal resources in
the DR Google Cloud project,

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.700
just enough to enable
the recovery scenario.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.760
When a disaster occurs,
the fail over strategy

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.030
requires a mirror of the
production environment

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.310
to be started in Google Cloud.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.060
Let's see how this would work.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.340
We create a VPC network, then
we configure the connectivity

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:14.200
between the on premise network
and the Google Cloud network.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.110
We need a Cloud Storage
bucket as the target

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.060
for our backup data.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.050
Now, for your on premise machine
to upload database backups

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.770
to Google Cloud, we will
need a service account key

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.700
to authenticate your machine
within an automated script.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.060
We would use IM policies
to restrict access

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.630
to the right user as well
as ensure that the service

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.630
account has the minimal
permissions required

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:45.280
and make sure uploads and
downloads to and from the cloud

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.840
storage bucket are working.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.220
Then, you finally script
the data transfer, create

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.980
a scheduled task
to run that script,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.840
and then create
custom images that

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.660
are configured for
each type of server

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.190
in the production environment.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.840
You configure the DNS to
point to your internet facing

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.380
web services and then create
the deployment manager

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.560
template that will create
servers in your Google Cloud

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:16.360
network using the previously
configured custom images.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.210
When a disaster
hits, all you need

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.340
is to execute the
deployment manager template,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.280
which will create a Google
Cloud deployment automatically.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.380
Apply the most recent database
backups and transaction

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.730
logs from the cloud
storage bucket,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.410
test the application works
as expected by simulating

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.230
a user in the recovered
environment, then

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:43.780
finally point the Cloud DNS to
the web server on Google Cloud.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.650
When the production environment
is running on premise again

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:51.100
and the environment can
support production workloads,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.370
we reverse the steps
that we followed.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.950
Typically it goes
something like this.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.080
Take a backup and
transaction logs

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.750
of the database running
on Google Cloud.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.870
Copy and apply the backup
files to the database

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.330
in production environment.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.240
At this stage, you should
prevent connections

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.430
to the application
in Google Cloud,

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:15.460
or you can stop the connections
after the database had

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.120
been successfully restored and
apply any transactions that

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.580
occurred after the backup
was taken In our case,

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.900
we need to stop the connections
to the global load balancer.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.760
From this point,
your application

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.510
will be unavailable until you
finish restoring the production

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:32.890
environment.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:37.600
Configure Cloud DNS to point
to your on premise web service.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.470
Ensure that the process you
had in place to copy data

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.770
to cloud storage is
operating as expected.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.740
Then finally delete
your deployments.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.280
Well, there you have it.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.610
If you are running your
application on premise

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.720
and have a constrained budget
and can work with higher RTO

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.240
and RPO values, then a cold
DR pattern is the way to go.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.140
We learned how to
approach recovering

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:05.800
the environment from failure
in a cold DR scenario.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.020
That's all for today on
GitHub Cooking in Cloud.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.890
Here's hoping you're cooking
up your own DR strategy.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.260
Join us next time where
we will share the recipe

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:19.270
to implement a warm standby DR
pattern for mainstream thought.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.400
If you would like to
see more such content,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.840
don't forget to like and
